{
  "configuration_id": "config_015",
  "reason": "Evidence: config_007 (scvi_n200, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) is the clear best at 0.706 acc, far ahead of the runner-up config_020 (pca_n2000, avgpool, weighted) at 0.635. With pca_n2000, transformer aggregation was worse than avgpool (0.586 vs 0.635), but scvi_n200 produces a learned, structured latent representation that may interact differently with attention-based aggregation than a linear PCA projection. Question: Does transformer aggregation unlock additional gains when paired with the stronger scvi_n200 encoder, potentially pushing accuracy above 0.706? This is the single most informative untested extension of the current best configuration—changing only the aggregation while keeping the proven best RNA encoder, image encoder, and fusion. Why this experiment: It directly exploits the strongest evidence (scvi_n200 + weighted fusion) while resolving the key remaining uncertainty about aggregation. If it beats 0.706, we have a new best; if not, it confirms avgpool is optimal and frees remaining budget for other directions (e.g., singleprofile or resnet50). Expected cost: moderate, roughly 5–6 GPU hours (transformer aggregation adds attention overhead over avgpool, but scvi_n200 is lighter than scgpt), well within the remaining budget of ~3 experiments."
}

